# Workflows from earlier work in this repository

Reusable steps learned from earlier tasks here. Values your task states are filled in; blanks in braces stand for the rest of your task's own names and values. Places are found in the code as it is now (current line numbers); the lines shown are excerpts.

## 1. Document a new rule (doc sources, Rules.md, README)
Filled from your task: {alias} = `no-duplicate-heading`, {tag} = `# Install`
Blanks: {rule_id} the rule's identifier, e.g. MDxxx; {rule_file} lowercase module/doc name of the rule; {description} the rule's description; {param} a rule parameter with type, default and meaning
1. Create the hand-written doc body {rule_file}.md (only the explanatory text starting at 'This rule is triggered when ...', with a markdown example, how parameters change behaviour, defaults, and a Rationale/Fixes section if sibling docs have one).
   a new file in `doc-build/`, named like the 55 there that start with `md`; shortest first: md039.md (14 lines), md018.md (20 lines), md019.md (21 lines), md027.md (21 lines), md020.md (22 lines), md033.md (22 lines). The shortest, `doc-build/md039.md`:
   ```
       1| This rule is triggered on links that have spaces surrounding the link text:
       2| 
       3| ```markdown
       4| [ a link ](https://www.example.com/)
       5| ```
       6| 
       7| To fix this, remove the spaces surrounding the link text:
       8| 
       9| ```markdown
      10| [a link](https://www.example.com/)
      11| ```
      12| 
      13| Rationale: Consistent formatting makes it easier to understand a document.
   ```
2. Create the generated-style doc {rule_file}.md: title `# `{rule_id}` - {description}`, Tags, Aliases, Parameters list (`{param}`: description (`type`, default `x`)), followed by the body. Normally produced by the docs build; confirm it exists after running `npm run build-docs`, create/fix by hand otherwise.
   a new file in `doc/`, named like the 55 there that start with `md`; shortest first: md039.md (22 lines), md018.md (28 lines), md019.md (29 lines), md020.md (30 lines), md011.md (31 lines), md021.md (32 lines)
3. Ensure doc/Rules.md has the rule's section (`<a name="{rule_file}"></a>` heading, tags, aliases, parameters, body) in numeric order; normally regenerated by the docs build.
4. Add the rule to the README rules list: `- **[{rule_id}](doc/{rule_file}.md)** *no-duplicate-heading* - {description}`.
5. Add {rule_id} to the README line listing the rules for tag # Install: find the line starting `- **`# Install`** - ` in README.md and append the rule id, keeping numeric order.
6. Run the docs build and confirm the generated files match what you wrote.

## 2. Add a new rule to the config schema and generated config files
Filled from your task: {tag} = `# Install`
Blanks: {rule_id} the rule's identifier; {alias} the rule's alias; {description} the rule's description; {param} each parameter with type, bounds, default, description
1. Add a `case "{rule_id}":` in the rule switch of the schema builder that defines each parameter's description, type, minimum/maximum, default (arrays: items type and default []), then break. (the rule has parameters)
   `schema/build-config-schema.mjs:92-691` in for (const rule of rules) { › switch (ruleName) {
   ```
      92|   switch (ruleName) {
        ⋮
     685|         },
     686|         "default": []
     687|       };
     688|       break;
     689|     default:
     690|       break;
     691|   }
   ```
2. Run `npm run build-config` (and `npm run build-docs` if needed) to regenerate schema files, then check each generated place contains the rule and the tag description lists include the rule id.
3. Check the {rule_id} entry in the sample config (JSONC) with description link comment and parameters with defaults. (regeneration did not add it)
   `schema/.markdownlint.jsonc:2-363` in {
   ```
       2| {
        ⋮
     357|   "MD062": {
     358|     // Maximum number of lines (0 for no limit)
     359|     "maximum": 0,
     360|     // List of languages to ignore
     361|     "ignore_languages": []
     362|   }
     363| }
   ```
4. Check the {rule_id} entry in the sample config (YAML). (regeneration did not add it)
5. Check the {rule_id} property in the strict schema and the # Install tag description listing the rule.
   `schema/markdownlint-config-schema-strict.json:6-5318` in { › "properties": {
   ```
       6|   "properties": {
        ⋮
    5312|             "warning"
    5313|           ]
    5314|         }
    5315|       ],
    5316|       "default": true
    5317|     }
    5318|   },
   ```
6. Check the {rule_id} property in the non-strict schema.
   `schema/markdownlint-config-schema.json:6-5318` in { › "properties": {
   ```
       6|   "properties": {
        ⋮
    5312|             "warning"
    5313|           ]
    5314|         }
    5315|       ],
    5316|       "default": true
    5317|     }
    5318|   },
   ```
7. In lib/configuration-strict.d.ts, check the ConfigurationStrict interface has the {rule_id} entry (doc comment with link, then the property) and that the tag comment (`* # Install : ...`) lists the rule id. (typings were not regenerated)

## 3. Update hard-coded test counts after adding a rule
1. Increase the project-file count assertion (all files) by the number of files added (observed +1 per new rule).
   `test/markdownlint-test-project.mjs:20-35` in test.suite(import.meta.url.replace(/^.*?\/(?<name>[^/]*)$/u, "$<nam... › test("projectFiles", (t) => {
   ```
      20|   test("projectFiles", (t) => {
        ⋮
      29|       const expected = {};
      30|       for (const file of files) {
      31|         expected[file] = [];
      32|       }
      33|       t.assert.deepEqual(actual, expected, "Issue(s) with project files.");
      34|     });
      35|   });
   ```
2. Increase the filtered-files count assertion likewise (+1 per new rule).
   `test/markdownlint-test-project.mjs:37-60` in test.suite(import.meta.url.replace(/^.*?\/(?<name>[^/]*)$/u, "$<nam... › test("projectFilesExtendedAscii", (t) => {
   ```
      37|   test("projectFilesExtendedAscii", (t) => {
        ⋮
      54|       const expected = {};
      55|       for (const file of filteredFiles) {
      56|         expected[file] = [];
      57|       }
      58|       t.assert.deepEqual(actual, expected, "Issue(s) with project files.");
      59|     });
      60|   });
   ```
3. Increase t.plan in the readme test (observed +2 per rule).
   `test/markdownlint-test.mjs:1020-1094` in test.suite(import.meta.url.replace(/^.*?\/(?<name>[^/]*)$/u, "$<nam... › test("readme", async(t) => {
   ```
    1020|   test("readme", async(t) => {
        ⋮
    1088|     const ruleLeft = rulesLeft.shift();
    1089|     t.assert.equal(!ruleLeft, true,
    1090|       "Missing rule documentation for " +
    1091|         (ruleLeft || "[NO RULE]").toString() + ".");
    1092|     const tagLeft = Object.keys(tagToRules).shift();
    1093|     t.assert.equal(!tagLeft, true, "Undocumented tag " + tagLeft + ".");
    1094|   });
   ```
4. Increase t.plan in the strict-schema validation test (observed +3).
   `test/markdownlint-test.mjs:1096-1132` in test.suite(import.meta.url.replace(/^.*?\/(?<name>[^/]*)$/u, "$<nam... › test("validateJsonUsingConfigSchemaStrict", async(t) => {
   ```
    1096|   test("validateJsonUsingConfigSchemaStrict", async(t) => {
        ⋮
    1126|           !!result,
    1127|           true,
    1128|           `${file}\n${JSON.stringify(validateSchemaStrict.errors, null, 2)}`
    1129|         );
    1130|       }
    1131|     }
    1132|   });
   ```
5. Increase t.plan in the built-in-rules-valid-URL test (observed +3).
   `test/markdownlint-test.mjs:1226-1240` in test.suite(import.meta.url.replace(/^.*?\/(?<name>[^/]*)$/u, "$<nam... › test("allBuiltInRulesHaveValidUrl", (t) => {
   ```
    1226|   test("allBuiltInRulesHaveValidUrl", (t) => {
        ⋮
    1234|       t.assert.equal(
    1235|         // @ts-ignore
    1236|         rule.information.href,
    1237|         `${packageJson.homepage}/blob/v${packageJson.version}/doc/${ruleName}.md`
    1238|       );
    1239|     }
    1240|   });
   ```
6. Increase t.plan in the version-numbers test (observed +8).
   `test/markdownlint-test.mjs:1593-1623` in test.suite(import.meta.url.replace(/^.*?\/(?<name>[^/]*)$/u, "$<nam... › test("version numbers match", async(t) => {
   ```
    1593|   test("version numbers match", async(t) => {
        ⋮
    1617|       const firstChangelogRe = /## (\d+\.\d+\.\d+)/u;
    1618|       match = firstChangelogRe.exec(content);
    1619|       if (match) {
    1620|         t.assert.equal(match[1], packageJson.version);
    1621|       }
    1622|     }
    1623|   });
   ```
7. Run the full tests; if a count still mismatches, use the 'expected N but received M' message to set exact values.
Watch out: After adding a rule, several hard-coded counts in the tests (file counts and t.plan values in four tests) go stale; fixing only the ones reported first costs repeated slow full test runs. Update all count places at once, using the 'plan expected X but received Y' messages.

## Last: check it all with one command
When all your edits are in, run once: `npm run lint; npm run build-docs; npm run build-config; npm test; git status --short`
These are the checks earlier runs of these workflows passed, in one call. Its output is long: keep its last 60 lines (`2>&1 | tail -n 60` in Bash, `2>&1 | Select-Object -Last 60` in PowerShell), or Claude Code saves it to a file you then have to read.
In those runs the checks rewrote these test snapshots, as expected for this kind of change: `test/markdownlint-test-scenarios.mjs.snapshot`.

