## 1. Create a built-in rule module and register it
Filled from your task, change by change: 1: {alias} = `list-max-depth`, {tag} = `bullet`; 2: {alias} = `maximum`, {tag} = `npm test`; 3: {alias} = `npm test`, {tag} = `npm run lint`; 4: {alias} = `maximum`, {tag} = `npm test`
Blanks: {rule_id} the rule's identifier, e.g. MDxxx; {rule_file} lowercase module name of the rule, e.g. mdxxx; {description} the rule's description text
1. Create the rule module {rule_file}.mjs: `// @ts-check` header, imports from ../helpers (helpers.cjs, micromark-helpers.cjs) and ./cache.mjs (filterByTypesCached), and `export default` with names [ {rule_id}, {alias} ], description, tags [ {tag} ], parser "micromark", and a function (params, onError) that reads parameters from params.config with defaults and reports via addError/addErrorDetailIf on the required line with the required detail text. Model it on the shortest existing rule module.
   a new file in `lib/`, named like the 53 there that start with `md`; shortest first: md035.mjs (24 lines), md061.mjs (28 lines), md047.mjs (29 lines), md046.mjs (32 lines), md001.mjs (33 lines), md040.mjs (33 lines). The shortest, `lib/md035.mjs`:
   ```
       1| // @ts-check
       2| 
       3| import { addErrorDetailIf } from "../helpers/helpers.cjs";
       4| import { filterByTypesCached } from "./cache.mjs";
       5| 
       6| /** @type {import("markdownlint").Rule} */
       7| export default {
       8|   "names": [ "MD035", "hr-style" ],
       9|   "description": "Horizontal rule style",
      10|   "tags": [ "hr" ],
      11|   "parser": "micromark",
      12|   "function": function MD035(params, onError) {
      13|     let style = String(params.config.style || "consistent").trim();
      14|     const thematicBreaks = filterByTypesCached([ "thematicBreak" ]);
      15|     for (const token of thematicBreaks) {
      16|       const { startLine, text } = token;
      17|       if (style === "consistent") {
      18|         style = text;
      19|       }
      20|       addErrorDetailIf(onError, startLine, style, text);
      21|     }
      22|   }
      23| };
   ```
2. Import the new module in the rules list imports, in numeric order after the previous rule's import.
   `lib/rules.mjs:3-59` the top-level `import …` statements
   ```
        ⋮
      53| import md055 from "./md055.mjs";
      54| import md056 from "./md056.mjs";
      55| import md058 from "./md058.mjs";
      56| import md059 from "./md059.mjs";
      57| import md060 from "./md060.mjs";
      58| import md061 from "./md061.mjs";
      59| import md062 from "./md062.mjs";
   ```
3. In the same file lib/rules.mjs, add the imported rule to the `const rules = [ ... ];` array, after the last existing rule.
4. Keep the code within the lint rules (e.g. no `continue` statements, handle possibly-undefined values such as an absent code-fence language before calling string methods).
Watch out: The lint config forbids `continue` statements (no-continue) and strict null checks apply to optional values such as a code fence language; write rule loops with if-blocks/filter instead, and run npm run lint before the full test.
Watch out: Ad-hoc debug scripts that use /tmp paths fail on this Windows environment (path resolves to C:\tmp\...). Use paths inside the repo or pass strings directly to the lint API, and remove scratch files afterwards.

